1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,380
Well, good morning, Canvas Community Church.

2
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:11,980
So excited to be here.

3
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:16,420
If you are a middle or high school student, you can be dismissed to the youth room to

4
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:17,940
hang out with Jarrett and the team.

5
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:20,660
For those of you that don't know me, my name is Ryan.

6
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:25,760
I'm one of the pastors here, and it is only by the grace of God that we are here this

7
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:26,000
morning.

8
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:33,960
There have been many dark days for most of us in this room when it comes to our church

9
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:38,460
experience, and there were probably days or weeks or even months where we weren't sure

10
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:42,900
what God was doing and didn't know what was to come.

11
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,980
But thanks to the Spirit of God moving in some pretty incredible ways throughout individuals

12
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:55,360
in both congregations, throughout an incredible amount of humility, of a willingness to lay

13
00:00:55,360 --> 00:01:00,820
down positions of power, a willingness to lay down preferences.

14
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:09,400
We have gotten here today, merging two congregations to become one for the sole purpose of us being

15
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,340
a kingdom outpost in our city.

16
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:17,000
We want to be here present and active in the city together.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,520
And God has done so many incredible things this morning that we are so excited about.

18
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:27,240
I could spend the rest of this sermon both giving thanks and glory to God for making this happen

19
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:31,320
and going down a list of individuals that made it possible.

20
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:37,940
I was talking to Kent the other week, and he said, last week, he said, it was 365 days ago

21
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:40,860
this week that I met Matt for the first time.

22
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:46,540
So, remember this, if you're ever voting on whether or not your church should bring on an interim pastor

23
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:51,400
who used to be a college professor, he might take you for a ride that you weren't expecting.

24
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,300
But we are so thankful to Matt.

25
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:55,380
We miss him and his family.

26
00:01:55,380 --> 00:02:01,920
And again, I could go through a list of everybody that made this happen, but today our focus should be on Christ.

27
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:11,160
And so we are launching a series that we're entitling My Part Matters, where we're going to spend the next seven weeks or so

28
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:17,340
talking about how we as individuals can take the commands of Scripture seriously.

29
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:24,100
And ultimately, that if we, each one of us, take the commands of Scripture seriously, we prioritize unity,

30
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:33,240
we prioritize brotherly and sisterly affection, that this merger can be successful, and we can see God do some incredible things.

31
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:41,660
But before we jump into a series called My Part Matters, I have to preface it, and we'll work this through the entire series.

32
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:47,300
But the entire series is predicated upon one really important anchoring truth,

33
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:52,680
and that is that your part only matters if it's submitted to his part.

34
00:02:54,100 --> 00:03:00,060
Our individual obedience only matters if we are following after what God is doing.

35
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,780
It reminds me of the psalmist who says,

36
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:06,520
If the Lord doesn't build the house, then the builders build in vain.

37
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,700
It reminds me of Jesus talking to his followers when he said,

38
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:13,800
I will build my church, and the gates of hell will not prevail against it.

39
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,240
He calls us to faithfulness.

40
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,720
He calls us to obedience.

41
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:21,520
But our obedience only matters if it's submitted to his will.

42
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:29,060
We can be the most organized, efficient, excellent organization with the clearest vision on earth.

43
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:32,860
And if we aren't following God, we are wasting our time.

44
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:35,840
So, we're going to talk about our part.

45
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:39,720
We're going to talk about what God's calling us to as individual believers to be obedient.

46
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:44,420
But let's remember that all of that is submitted to what he is doing in this place,

47
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:47,580
what he is doing in this city.

48
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:51,880
Let me pray, and then we'll hop into a text of Scripture.

49
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:56,800
Father, we are so thankful that you brought us here to this place this morning.

50
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:03,100
There were dark days and weeks and months where I had no clue what you were doing.

51
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:08,980
I was confused and lost.

52
00:04:09,980 --> 00:04:14,940
And like you always have in my life, you've proved your faithfulness again and again.

53
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:19,340
There's been evidence of your faithfulness all over my life, all over our life,

54
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:23,540
all over the lives of our churches and congregations, and you were doing a new thing.

55
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,680
And we're so thankful for that thing.

56
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:32,000
Even some of us in this room that didn't want this, wouldn't have chosen this.

57
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,780
May we have confidence that you're in it and we lay down our preferences for the sake of what you're doing in our midst.

58
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:45,120
So God, just be present from this day forward.

59
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:54,380
May your Holy Spirit lead, guide, and direct each of us to a common goal with common unity for your glory and for our good.

60
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,600
In Jesus' name I pray. Amen.

61
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:04,280
So you have your Bibles and you want to follow along with me with a paperback or on the screen or on your phone.

62
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:06,000
We're going to be in Acts chapter 2.

63
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,420
We're going to get to Acts chapter 2 in just a few moments.

64
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:15,900
But I am, believe it or not, young enough to remember with some clarity my engagement process.

65
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:18,900
I remember some details around it.

66
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,920
I was a young kid leaving a conservative Bible college.

67
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:25,940
And I had this deep desire to be a godly husband.

68
00:05:25,940 --> 00:05:35,220
I had this deep desire to have a godly marriage and to enter into marriage with this foundation of building a godly family.

69
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:43,280
And I distinctly remember this process going into marriage where I was, like, imagining, dreaming about what my marriage would be like.

70
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:48,060
Dreaming about how I could be a godly husband and how my wife and I, we could be a godly couple

71
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:52,720
and how we might build our lives on the foundation of a godly family.

72
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:59,440
And I was reading every single marriage book and listening to every single marriage sermon that I could get my hands on.

73
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:04,380
And I distinctly remember in this process of imagining what my marriage would look like.

74
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:07,160
Making a checklist of behaviors.

75
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:13,200
Carbon copying the behaviors that these other pastors and authors were telling me that I should do.

76
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:17,400
I remember making this long list of things that I was going to do every day.

77
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:22,780
There were 40-year-old, or pastors who had been pastors for 40 years, married for 40 years, saying,

78
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:25,440
I never miss a day praying for my wife.

79
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:31,480
I put sticky notes, handwritten sticky notes, love notes on the mirror every single day.

80
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,880
All of these things that were like, I was going to do these things.

81
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:42,900
I remember this one pastor said that him and his wife, on their honeymoon, sat down and created a mission statement for their family for the rest of their life.

82
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:46,660
And I was like, this is the advice that I was putting into practice.

83
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:48,200
Like, I'm going to do these things.

84
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:54,320
And about six months into my marriage, I did a little self-evaluating.

85
00:06:55,060 --> 00:07:01,460
And realized that I had pretty much missed every single checkbox on the list at some point or time.

86
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:06,660
And it wasn't that I had totally, it wasn't that I was totally unsuccessful at being a godly husband.

87
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:11,240
But it was, I was focusing on the wrong things.

88
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:20,160
I was trying to copy behaviors of other people, rather than just being driven by the core attitudes that they were expounding in those behaviors.

89
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:31,220
See, I was so focused on the specific tasks that other husbands were doing, that I missed the overarching attitudes that should drive me to be a godly husband.

90
00:07:31,220 --> 00:07:33,080
And I share this story with you this morning.

91
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:36,760
Because I believe we're at a similar place as a church.

92
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:44,340
Where we can begin to imagine a church that God uses in some incredible ways in this city.

93
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:53,560
That God uses in each of your families to draw us closer to him and to send us out to have an impact, to share the hope and healing message of Jesus to our community.

94
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:56,980
But what we might do is we might get lost in the tasks.

95
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:00,580
We might get lost in the specifics.

96
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:06,540
So much so that we miss the core attitudes of what a healthy and effective church looks like.

97
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:13,920
And so this morning what I want to do, rather than get lost in some tasks or behaviors that might set us up for failures,

98
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:21,260
I just want to look at four core overarching attitudes of a healthy and effective church.

99
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:29,100
And at the end, at the end of this message, my goal is that we would imagine a church built on these four core principles,

100
00:08:29,100 --> 00:08:33,780
these four core attitudes that we're going to draw out of Acts chapter 2.

101
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:35,200
Does that sound like a good plan?

102
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:41,840
All right, I usually ask that question and I usually don't care what you say because it's already the plan.

103
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:46,500
So maybe one day I'll ask, you'll say no and I'll change the plan, but for now, let's go.

104
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:51,900
All right, so Acts chapter 2, starting in verse 41, says this,

105
00:08:51,900 --> 00:09:20,860
A deep sense of awe came over all of them and the apostles performed many miraculous signs and wonders and all the believers met together in one place and shared everything that they had.

106
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:25,700
They sold their property and possessions and shared money with those in need.

107
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:33,880
They worshiped together at the temple each day and they met in their homes for the Lord's supper and they shared meals with great joy and generosity,

108
00:09:33,880 --> 00:09:38,600
all the while praising God and enjoying the goodwill of all people.

109
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:44,660
And each day, the Lord added to their fellowship, those who were being saved.

110
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,300
This is the word of the Lord.

111
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:46,840
Amen.

112
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:56,360
So, I want to give a couple points of context before we get into our four core attitudes of a healthy and effective church.

113
00:09:56,460 --> 00:10:02,900
The first point of context that I want to share is I included, you'll notice I included the verse before the paragraph break.

114
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:08,320
If you've got headings in your Bible, you might notice that I included the verse before the heading.

115
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:15,460
And that's because I think it helps us to feel that some of the same things that the new believers were feeling.

116
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:26,040
See, up until verse 41, when Peter kind of concludes his first sermon to this large group of people, the New Testament church was made up of about 120 people.

117
00:10:26,760 --> 00:10:34,740
120 people of shared experience, shared culture, they kind of knew each other, they understood one another.

118
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:40,100
And overnight, in an instant, 3,000 people were added to their fellowship.

119
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:45,140
New faces, new cultures, new ideas, new perspectives.

120
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:49,660
And you can imagine how that might feel to a group of people that were comfortable where they were,

121
00:10:49,660 --> 00:10:52,500
that were comfortable with the group that they were in.

122
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:56,120
And now, all of a sudden, there's people that they might be skeptical of, might be confused about,

123
00:10:56,120 --> 00:10:59,260
might not understand their cultural background and upbringing.

124
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:01,520
Does anybody feel similar?

125
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:04,440
The numbers are a little bit different, right?

126
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:05,440
Numbers are a little bit different.

127
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:11,080
But both of us, we were in a group of people that we were comfortable with, that we felt good about,

128
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:13,780
that we had done life with and shared experiences with.

129
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:16,860
We had common language and a common understanding.

130
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:20,400
And now we're thrown together in this place under this single roof.

131
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:22,480
And God's called us to be a family together.

132
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:25,360
But if we're honest, we might be skeptical of one another.

133
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:30,600
We might not fully understand some of the language that we use or some of the culture that exists.

134
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:39,780
And yet, it's in that context that through the Holy Spirit, God was able to craft this incredibly unified church.

135
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:42,700
There's a point of context that I think is helpful.

136
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:47,480
There's two more points of context that I want to point out before we hop into our four core attributes.

137
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:54,680
The first point of context is that this paragraph here sounded pretty incredible, didn't it?

138
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:56,560
Sounded like something that you'd want to be a part of.

139
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:01,480
I could pretty much just read this text and walk off stage, and you could say, like, that sounds awesome.

140
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:03,460
I'd love to be a part of a church that's like that.

141
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:10,520
The first point of context is that this passage is a summary statement of the church at its best.

142
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:19,540
Like, you read the rest of the book of Acts, and it's filled with the church failing, messing up, being mean to each other, lying to the Holy Spirit.

143
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:23,260
Like, there's all kinds of stories of the church failing.

144
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:28,200
But this is a paragraph, this is a summary statement of the church at its best.

145
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:35,300
This is what, when you step back over time and see the Holy Spirit working through a church, this was Luke's summary statement.

146
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:44,660
There's actually two more paragraphs very similar throughout Acts, where Luke is saying, man, when we were doing it, when we were doing the thing, when we were obedient, when we were faithful, this is what it looked like.

147
00:12:44,660 --> 00:12:47,980
And then throughout the rest of the book, he adds in all of the times they didn't do it.

148
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:53,380
They got focused on themselves, their own preferences, their fears, their worries, their doubts, all of that.

149
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:57,600
And so let's remember that this is the church at its best.

150
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,680
We're going to fail.

151
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:02,680
We're going to step on each other's toes.

152
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:04,700
We're going to make each other mad.

153
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:09,140
We're going to probably gossip behind each other's backs at times.

154
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:10,440
We're going to mess up.

155
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:14,800
And that doesn't mean that we aren't a successful and healthy church.

156
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:15,880
That just means we're human.

157
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:21,400
The church is full of people getting in the way of God's plan.

158
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,640
But imagine a church.

159
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:28,740
Imagine a church that in the midst of their failure and brokenness is submitted to the Spirit.

160
00:13:28,740 --> 00:13:34,060
May we step back a year from now and have a similar summary statement be true of us.

161
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:37,100
So it's the church at its best.

162
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:40,640
And secondly, it's descriptive, not prescriptive.

163
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:44,440
Luke is describing what the church in the first century was doing.

164
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:48,300
He's not saying this is what the church throughout the rest of history should do specifically.

165
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:56,140
And so rather than getting lost in the behaviors of the early church, we're going to look at the core attitudes that they built their foundation on.

166
00:13:56,140 --> 00:13:57,380
Does that make sense?

167
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:11,180
All right, so core attitude number one, the early church in a healthy, I believe a healthy and vibrant church will be built on the foundation of being committed to core doctrine, a healthy and vibrant church.

168
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:16,780
And I realized after I wrote this message that there's probably a sermon for each one of these points.

169
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:21,320
So I'm just going to do my best to give you the cliff notes, summarized version.

170
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:24,980
If I missed something, I'm sorry, but I wrote four sermons and put it into one.

171
00:14:24,980 --> 00:14:25,540
So here we are.

172
00:14:25,540 --> 00:14:29,880
The early church and a healthy and vibrant church will be committed to core doctrine.

173
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:34,260
It says all the believers devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching.

174
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:36,960
So there was no Bible at this point.

175
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:39,040
They had the Old Testament in the temple.

176
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:46,380
They had it kind of memorized and they understood the Old Testament, but they didn't have a phone with a Bible app.

177
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:47,600
They didn't have the printing press.

178
00:14:47,600 --> 00:14:50,540
And so they devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching.

179
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:54,980
The apostles are people that had eyewitness accounts with Jesus.

180
00:14:54,980 --> 00:14:57,080
And so what are they doing?

181
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:58,700
They're clarifying the Old Testament.

182
00:14:58,700 --> 00:15:03,880
See, much of the nation of Israel had gotten the message of the Old Testament distorted.

183
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:08,780
They'd gotten lost in the weeds and they missed the fact that it was all pointing to Jesus.

184
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:15,400
And Jesus was standing right in front of them and they were quoting to him the Old Testament about why he couldn't be the Messiah.

185
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:19,220
And so the apostles, they're clarifying this for the early church.

186
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:21,480
They're saying, no, the Old Testament is all about Jesus.

187
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:23,300
Don't get lost in the weeds.

188
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:29,600
Don't get so focused on circumcision that you don't let the Gentiles come and partake in the thing that Jesus is doing.

189
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:33,520
Don't get so lost in the weeds that you missed that it's all about Jesus.

190
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:42,320
And so the apostles, they're teaching how the Old Testament all points to the central focus, which is the Messiah, Jesus.

191
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:46,620
And not only that, but they were there when Jesus was teaching.

192
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:49,120
And so they're expounding on the teachings of Jesus.

193
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:51,000
They're expounding on the Sermon on the Mount.

194
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,060
They are making the central focus of their doctrine Jesus.

195
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:03,940
So they were devoting themselves to this really simplified core doctrine centered around Jesus.

196
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:12,500
Paul later would write this to the 1 Corinthians in 1 Corinthians 15 about this core doctrine, this thing that they stayed focused on.

197
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:15,920
He said, I pass on to you what was most important.

198
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:18,040
This is their core doctrine.

199
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:21,320
Paul says, this is what was passed on to me.

200
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:27,120
That Christ died for our sins just as the Scriptures, just as the Old Testament said.

201
00:16:27,640 --> 00:16:32,520
And he was buried and he was raised from the dead on the third day, just as the Scriptures said.

202
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:39,000
They devoted themselves to this core doctrine, that Jesus was at the center of everything.

203
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:45,320
I think if we're going to be a healthy, vibrant expression of the church in this place, we've got to get this right.

204
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:51,860
We've got to be committed and focused on core doctrine that puts Jesus at the center.

205
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:56,840
This doesn't mean that secondary theology isn't important.

206
00:16:56,840 --> 00:17:04,900
It does mean that we prioritize relationships with our brothers and sisters in Christ above and beyond secondary doctrine.

207
00:17:04,900 --> 00:17:11,260
This is one of my favorite things that I've learned about the brethren as I've kind of dived into studying the history of the brethren.

208
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:12,800
This is a very brethren value.

209
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:15,940
Their doctrine, their core doctrine, has Jesus at the center.

210
00:17:15,940 --> 00:17:21,960
And it's not that they don't have positions on secondary theology, but it's that they prioritize.

211
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:23,300
It's in the name, brethren.

212
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:29,500
We're committed to coming together, to prioritizing relationship in the midst of secondary theology.

213
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:32,280
I'm going to start stepping on some toes, but don't worry.

214
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:34,220
I'm an equal opportunity ascender.

215
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:37,060
I'll step on everybody's toes, including my own before the day is over.

216
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:42,980
Some of you in this room, you've had continual fallout in your church experience.

217
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:48,940
And it's because you've elevated non-essential doctrine to the level of essential doctrine.

218
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:53,780
You've become religious and judgmental about secondary things.

219
00:17:53,780 --> 00:17:56,360
And people don't want to be around you.

220
00:17:56,360 --> 00:17:58,860
It's true.

221
00:17:58,860 --> 00:17:59,880
People don't want to be around you.

222
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:00,660
And here's the reality.

223
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:02,400
You're probably wrong anyway.

224
00:18:02,400 --> 00:18:06,240
I'm probably wrong anyway.

225
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:10,720
All of us one day are going to stand before God and have secondary theology corrected.

226
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:13,740
And if you think you won't, then you're missing the point.

227
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:20,580
So, we've got to keep core doctrine focused on Jesus.

228
00:18:22,340 --> 00:18:23,600
I'm an equal opportunity ascender.

229
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:30,680
Others of you, you've had bad experiences in the church community because you're unwilling to even agree to core doctrine.

230
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:34,240
Because it seems harsh or unloving.

231
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:42,040
And the reality is, we don't get to redefine terms to fit the things that make the most sense to us.

232
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:46,620
We can't deny core doctrine because we don't like the way that it sounds.

233
00:18:49,020 --> 00:18:55,320
So, may we be a people that prioritize relationships with one another, centered around core doctrine.

234
00:18:55,320 --> 00:18:57,600
That is Jesus at the center.

235
00:18:57,600 --> 00:19:03,420
Jesus says that the world is going to hate us because it hated him first.

236
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:04,760
We can't afford to hate one another.

237
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:08,260
We can't afford to argue with one another.

238
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:12,580
We can't afford to hate one another when the world is going to hate us.

239
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:14,180
Imagine a church that gets this right.

240
00:19:14,180 --> 00:19:17,780
Imagine a church that's unapologetically convinced of the gospel.

241
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:22,820
A church that cares more about obeying Jesus' commands than fighting amongst ourselves.

242
00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:24,760
A church that's burdened for the loss.

243
00:19:24,760 --> 00:19:27,100
Far surpasses its burden for being right.

244
00:19:27,100 --> 00:19:32,740
I believe that a church that is built on the foundations of individuals.

245
00:19:32,740 --> 00:19:34,400
Remember, this is my part matters.

246
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:39,040
We're not going to be a church that's committed to core doctrine if you don't get this right as individuals.

247
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:48,960
If we are individuals committed to Jesus at the center of our doctrine, willing to prioritize our relationships with brothers and sisters over our secondary theological preferences.

248
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:51,020
Imagine a church that gets this right.

249
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:59,920
Second, overarching core value I see in this text is this church that's dedicated to prayer.

250
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:09,880
It says that all the believers devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and to fellowship and to sharing their meals and to prayer.

251
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:15,180
This is one of the core attitudes that every Christian knows should exist in their life.

252
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:16,900
Right?

253
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:18,200
Like, this is the Sunday school answer.

254
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:19,780
We all know that prayer is important.

255
00:20:19,780 --> 00:20:23,860
But if we're honest, it's also one of the most difficult things for us to get right.

256
00:20:23,860 --> 00:20:25,860
Especially in America.

257
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:26,560
You guys know this?

258
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:27,640
I don't know if you've noticed this or not.

259
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:29,840
America is driven by productivity.

260
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:33,040
You guys ever feel that when you have conversations with your bosses at work?

261
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:35,200
Like, we're driven by productivity.

262
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:40,720
And if we're honest with ourselves, prayer doesn't always feel like the most productive thing.

263
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:49,520
However, I truly believe that out of these four attitudes that we're going to look at this morning, prayer is the fuel for the other three.

264
00:20:49,520 --> 00:21:00,260
If we don't, if we're not devoted to prayer, we have an epic, powerful car sitting in the driveway with no gasoline in the engine.

265
00:21:00,500 --> 00:21:08,740
Imagine you having the dream car of your life sitting in your driveway, but no fuel to take it off the driveway.

266
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:16,900
That is a church that has all of the other things going right, but is unwilling to devote themselves to prayer.

267
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:21,180
This isn't just individual prayer life.

268
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:22,960
This isn't just you and your time with God.

269
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:26,440
Some translations actually translate this as the prayers.

270
00:21:26,440 --> 00:21:30,060
Like, there's this corporate thing that they are doing together as a church.

271
00:21:30,060 --> 00:21:35,440
They are coming together to pray with one another, to pray for their community.

272
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:42,680
We should be dedicated to prayer in our individual lives and also as a church.

273
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:48,480
This merger is going to be as fruitful as we all are hoping and believing that it will be.

274
00:21:48,480 --> 00:21:51,700
It has to be built on the foundation of the prayers.

275
00:21:51,700 --> 00:21:54,340
Our prayers.

276
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:56,200
We've got to take this seriously.

277
00:21:56,200 --> 00:21:59,880
We've got to set aside time to be intentional with this.

278
00:21:59,880 --> 00:22:03,080
So I want to get really practical on this point.

279
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:11,080
And because I was writing the sermon, I picked some arbitrary times that I hope will be beneficial to you.

280
00:22:11,220 --> 00:22:17,740
But I'm going to put some times on the screen that I would just love to encourage you to set an alarm on your phone.

281
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:27,060
Or you'll see in the bulletin we put kind of a promo for our app, which will get – remember, tense reminder, patience, patience, patience.

282
00:22:27,060 --> 00:22:29,480
Our app right now is still named Canvas Church.

283
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:30,480
We will rename it.

284
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:31,180
It's a process.

285
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:40,420
But if you download our app, you can subscribe to a notification list where I will send you a notification every time throughout the week.

286
00:22:40,420 --> 00:22:41,460
And this is just an encouragement.

287
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:43,920
Again, let's not get lost in the behaviors.

288
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:46,780
If you can't make these times work, then set aside a different time.

289
00:22:46,780 --> 00:22:56,140
But I think there's something special about knowing when you go to pray that other people from your church are also praying at the same time for the same things.

290
00:22:56,140 --> 00:23:00,960
And I picked strategic times throughout the week so that we're kind of building this into the fabric of our schedule.

291
00:23:00,960 --> 00:23:06,300
And listen, this can be anything as easy as a shotgun prayer from behind the wheel on your way to work.

292
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:11,180
Or maybe at lunchtime, you've got time to intentionally fast for an hour at lunch and pray.

293
00:23:11,180 --> 00:23:20,080
Whatever it looks like for you, let's be a people that are devoted to prayer, both in our individual lives and corporately as a church.

294
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:28,060
Another hope that I have, and I've heard that Grace has done this in the past, is that we would have events where we would come here,

295
00:23:28,060 --> 00:23:35,280
where some of us meet in the parking lot and walk around the neighborhood and begin to pray for the people in our community, the people in our city.

296
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:44,020
May we be a church who is built on the foundation of being committed to core doctrine and being devoted to prayer.

297
00:23:44,920 --> 00:23:52,680
The third core attitude is, may we be a church who is built on the foundation of being submitted to the Holy Spirit.

298
00:23:54,780 --> 00:24:02,360
It says, a deep sense of awe came over all of them, and the apostles performed many miraculous signs and wonders,

299
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:08,020
and all the believers met together in one place and shared everything that they had.

300
00:24:10,120 --> 00:24:17,900
As I was preparing this message, I have to be honest with you, this is the one core attitude that I struggled with explaining the most.

301
00:24:17,900 --> 00:24:25,400
Because the last thing that I wanted to do was give a Christian-y, cliche answer about being submitted to the Holy Spirit.

302
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:32,180
And often that's what we do when we talk about the Holy Spirit in church, because it can be this mysterious, supernatural thing.

303
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:35,540
And so I wanted to figure out, like, how do I explain this?

304
00:24:35,540 --> 00:24:42,640
What does it look like for us to be a church that builds our expression of the church on individuals submitted to the Holy Spirit?

305
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:49,040
And depending on what theological background you come from, this could mean two very different things.

306
00:24:49,040 --> 00:24:54,000
And so, since I'm, you know, super humble and an excellent preacher.

307
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,120
Just kidding.

308
00:24:56,120 --> 00:24:57,320
I'm just kidding.

309
00:24:57,320 --> 00:24:57,880
That's a joke.

310
00:24:58,980 --> 00:25:04,060
I asked different people in our community, and I spent a lot of time really wrestling with it.

311
00:25:04,060 --> 00:25:05,020
I came up with two things.

312
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:06,120
This is a good preacher joke.

313
00:25:06,120 --> 00:25:10,500
They're two C's, because, you know, every preacher has to use the same first letter.

314
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:13,840
So, two elements of what it means to be submitted to the Holy Spirit.

315
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:18,260
The first is curious, and the second is common.

316
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:19,440
First, curious.

317
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:25,120
There is an undeniably supernatural element to submitting to the Holy Spirit.

318
00:25:25,120 --> 00:25:30,880
The text says, literally, there was an awe, a deep sense of awe that comes over them.

319
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:33,680
This isn't something that you can explain away in terms.

320
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:38,180
There was a sense of awe that the entire church was feeling as they were coming together,

321
00:25:38,180 --> 00:25:42,480
and the apostles performed many miraculous signs and wonders.

322
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:48,620
There is an undeniably supernatural element of individuals being submitted to the Holy Spirit.

323
00:25:48,620 --> 00:25:50,420
I love that song we sang, Make Room.

324
00:25:50,420 --> 00:25:53,500
That's the curious aspect of being submitted to the Spirit.

325
00:25:53,500 --> 00:25:58,080
May we come to God and say, God, I'm just making room for you to do whatever you want to do.

326
00:25:58,080 --> 00:26:04,280
I don't know what that looks like always, but may you do some miraculous things.

327
00:26:04,280 --> 00:26:10,420
Jesus tells his followers that it's better that he leaves so that the Holy Spirit can come.

328
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:14,760
Have you ever read that passage in John and just stopped and thought, how is that possible?

329
00:26:14,760 --> 00:26:20,460
How is it possible that Jesus says, it's better that I get out of here so that the Holy Spirit can come?

330
00:26:20,460 --> 00:26:26,780
Because when the Holy Spirit comes, he will fill individual believers who will be spread throughout the entire world

331
00:26:26,780 --> 00:26:32,540
in different languages and cultures and backgrounds, and they will be my witness to the ends of the earth.

332
00:26:32,540 --> 00:26:38,620
There's something incredibly supernatural about us being willing to let the Holy Spirit lead us.

333
00:26:40,540 --> 00:26:44,280
Jesus says another insane thing when he talks about the Holy Spirit.

334
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:47,080
He says, you will do even greater things than me.

335
00:26:47,080 --> 00:26:50,400
There's a supernatural element to this.

336
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:58,020
And so the reality is, I'm going to step on my conservative brothers and sisters' toes because it's me.

337
00:26:58,020 --> 00:26:58,980
This is where I come from.

338
00:26:58,980 --> 00:27:04,400
Some of us, we believe in a resurrected Savior, but not in his present resurrecting power.

339
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:11,140
If you found Jesus in a more conservative, intellectually driven church, I think you need to consider, possibly,

340
00:27:11,140 --> 00:27:17,460
stepping out of your comfort zone and being open, making room for the Holy Spirit to do something curious in your life.

341
00:27:17,460 --> 00:27:20,380
Scripture is full of examples of this.

342
00:27:20,380 --> 00:27:25,520
When I try and illustrate this to people that are wrestling with the concept,

343
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:30,100
I talk about a parent who has four presents wrapped under the tree,

344
00:27:30,100 --> 00:27:33,380
and the kids open the first three presents, and they're enamored with it.

345
00:27:33,380 --> 00:27:33,860
They love it.

346
00:27:33,860 --> 00:27:37,060
They're so excited about these gifts that their parent has given them,

347
00:27:37,060 --> 00:27:39,560
and they get lost in playing with the three gifts,

348
00:27:39,560 --> 00:27:44,300
and they kind of leave the other one underneath the tree.

349
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:52,860
I think those of us that are kind of concerned with what might happen if we open that Holy Spirit box,

350
00:27:53,260 --> 00:27:55,440
we just get consumed with playing with the three,

351
00:27:55,440 --> 00:27:58,580
and we miss out on something that God wants to do in our life.

352
00:27:58,580 --> 00:28:05,800
Let's be a church full of people who are open to the Holy Spirit doing some supernatural things in our life.

353
00:28:05,800 --> 00:28:07,500
Don't worry.

354
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:09,680
I told you I'm an equal opportunity offender.

355
00:28:09,680 --> 00:28:15,580
I believe that there is both a curious and a common aspect of being submitted to the Holy Spirit.

356
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:19,760
You see all throughout Scripture that when the Spirit is doing something, it's doing it in the body.

357
00:28:21,380 --> 00:28:27,500
The text seems to indicate that not only was there this supernatural sense of awe,

358
00:28:27,500 --> 00:28:34,420
but it was also the driving force behind unifying 3,000 people of different backgrounds

359
00:28:34,420 --> 00:28:37,240
and different ethnicities and different ideologies.

360
00:28:37,240 --> 00:28:43,560
The Holy Spirit was drawing them together, doing something common in an incredibly diverse community.

361
00:28:43,760 --> 00:28:49,140
The Spirit of God brings unity to an extremely diverse group of people.

362
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:55,220
And so you don't get to just do whatever you want to do and blame it on the Holy Spirit.

363
00:28:55,220 --> 00:28:59,880
The Holy Spirit should be doing something common in the body.

364
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:02,620
You should be affirmed by your brothers and sisters in Jesus.

365
00:29:02,780 --> 00:29:06,760
If you're stepping out and making room for the Spirit and the Spirit's doing a work in your life,

366
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:10,420
like other brothers and sisters in Christ should affirm that with Scripture.

367
00:29:10,420 --> 00:29:11,940
Right?

368
00:29:11,940 --> 00:29:16,660
So there's this curious and common aspect both.

369
00:29:16,660 --> 00:29:25,080
And when I was trying to think of how to illustrate this, I think, Grace, your vote is a great example of this.

370
00:29:25,160 --> 00:29:26,380
You all came to consensus.

371
00:29:26,380 --> 00:29:28,100
What does this mean?

372
00:29:28,100 --> 00:29:31,560
Does consensus mean that all of you were super stoked about doing this thing?

373
00:29:31,560 --> 00:29:35,780
Some of you were probably like, I don't know if I would choose this.

374
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:37,280
I don't know if this is what I think is right.

375
00:29:37,280 --> 00:29:41,900
But at the end of the day, you submitted to what the Holy Spirit was doing in the community.

376
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:46,820
This is what it means to be submitted to the Holy Spirit,

377
00:29:46,820 --> 00:29:53,520
that we're willing to lay down our preferences and what we think God might be doing to fit in with the broader group.

378
00:29:54,600 --> 00:29:57,180
You don't just get to be weird and blame it on the Holy Spirit.

379
00:29:57,180 --> 00:29:59,320
I'm sorry.

380
00:29:59,320 --> 00:30:03,680
And being submitted to the Holy Spirit doesn't mean you get to say a quick prayer

381
00:30:03,680 --> 00:30:05,560
and then do the thing that you want it to do anyway.

382
00:30:05,560 --> 00:30:10,700
I've seen far too many people, both conservatives and charismatics,

383
00:30:10,700 --> 00:30:14,580
that use the Holy Spirit as an excuse to do something really ungodly.

384
00:30:14,580 --> 00:30:17,260
We just can't do that.

385
00:30:17,260 --> 00:30:19,080
We've got to hold each other accountable.

386
00:30:19,080 --> 00:30:23,320
Imagine a church filled with individuals, submitted to the Holy Spirit,

387
00:30:23,320 --> 00:30:26,360
open to the curious, but accountable to the common.

388
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:33,900
Lastly, imagine a church that's invested in authentic biblical community.

389
00:30:33,900 --> 00:30:39,520
It says they sold their property and possessions and shared money with those in need.

390
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:42,880
They worshiped together in the temple each day, met in homes for the Lord's Supper,

391
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:46,140
and shared their meals with great joy and generosity,

392
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:50,740
all the while praising God and enjoying the goodwill of all the people.

393
00:30:50,740 --> 00:30:56,840
Imagine a church invested in authentic biblical community.

394
00:30:56,980 --> 00:30:58,440
It says my words carefully here.

395
00:30:58,440 --> 00:31:09,340
Both invested and authentic are two ways in which I think the church in America misses this value from time to time.

396
00:31:09,340 --> 00:31:10,480
First, invest it.

397
00:31:10,480 --> 00:31:16,620
It takes a tremendous amount of effort to build this level of community in a diverse group of people.

398
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:18,160
You have to be committed to it.

399
00:31:18,160 --> 00:31:19,780
You have to be invested in it.

400
00:31:19,820 --> 00:31:21,940
It takes time and effort.

401
00:31:21,940 --> 00:31:25,520
Not only that, but it has to be authentic.

402
00:31:25,520 --> 00:31:30,820
So many churches that I've been a part of, I've done this in my own life,

403
00:31:30,820 --> 00:31:35,940
we're willing to submit to this kind of shallow country club level community

404
00:31:35,940 --> 00:31:39,200
where we don't actually do life with one another or get to know one another.

405
00:31:39,200 --> 00:31:41,700
We just wave at each other on the way in and on the way out.

406
00:31:41,940 --> 00:31:46,460
We smile and say, Lord, bless your heart, and all of the weird Christian phrases that we say that don't make any sense.

407
00:31:46,460 --> 00:31:47,580
Like, this is what we do.

408
00:31:47,580 --> 00:31:52,300
And so imagine a church that gets both of these elements of community right,

409
00:31:52,300 --> 00:31:56,880
that they work hard to build it, that they're committed to it,

410
00:31:56,880 --> 00:32:00,820
and that they work hard to be vulnerable with one another, transparent with one another,

411
00:32:00,820 --> 00:32:02,820
actually do life with one another.

412
00:32:02,820 --> 00:32:06,460
This is one of the greatest apologetics of our time.

413
00:32:07,240 --> 00:32:14,300
If we're going to reach a city that is in a culture that is overwhelmed by loneliness,

414
00:32:14,300 --> 00:32:19,100
overwhelmed by thinking that nobody actually knows them for who they are,

415
00:32:19,100 --> 00:32:22,180
imagine if we got this right.

416
00:32:22,180 --> 00:32:27,480
Imagine if we did life together, and we were vulnerable with one another,

417
00:32:27,480 --> 00:32:30,520
and we weren't willing to settle for fake, shallow versions of it,

418
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:34,520
but we got to know each other, and we forgave one another in the midst of our brokenness.

419
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:41,780
Authentic biblical community is one of the most difficult yet rewarding aspects of the Christian faith.

420
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:44,220
It requires you to be flexible and available.

421
00:32:44,220 --> 00:32:47,320
It requires you to show up consistently and transparently.

422
00:32:47,320 --> 00:32:52,560
It requires you being honest with your struggles and refusing to be judgmental towards others

423
00:32:52,560 --> 00:32:54,820
when they are vulnerable with you about their struggles.

424
00:32:54,820 --> 00:33:04,400
I've got some good news and some bad news as we begin to wrap up our message.

425
00:33:04,460 --> 00:33:06,780
Which one would you guys prefer if we go first?

426
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:07,940
I'd love some crowd participation.

427
00:33:07,940 --> 00:33:10,220
You know what?

428
00:33:10,220 --> 00:33:11,680
I knew I liked you guys for a reason.

429
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:18,760
I'm a bad news first kind of guy because I tend to be a cup is half full kind of guy,

430
00:33:18,760 --> 00:33:23,640
and so I hear the bad news, and I do this thing in my mind where I convince myself that it's not as bad as it actually is,

431
00:33:23,640 --> 00:33:27,960
and then I get the good news on top of it, and my glass goes from half full to like three quarters full.

432
00:33:28,020 --> 00:33:29,320
It ends up working out really well.

433
00:33:29,320 --> 00:33:32,640
So, Rachel, we're the same person, aren't we?

434
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:33,740
Okay, good.

435
00:33:33,740 --> 00:33:37,820
So, we're going to go bad news first.

436
00:33:40,900 --> 00:33:47,140
The bad news is that the consistency thing, and we all know, we all have busy schedules, full calendars.

437
00:33:47,140 --> 00:33:50,500
The consistency part isn't even the hardest part.

438
00:33:50,500 --> 00:33:53,480
The hardest part is the authentic part.

439
00:33:54,420 --> 00:34:00,080
It is so hard to trust people enough to actually be open and transparent,

440
00:34:00,080 --> 00:34:03,140
to share your struggles and your failures,

441
00:34:03,140 --> 00:34:08,720
and to believe that they will extend love and grace rather than judgment and hypocrisy.

442
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:16,080
Many of us in this room, we've heard Canvas leadership use the word churchy.

443
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:21,180
We don't want to become churchy, and I think what we mean behind that phrase more than anything else is this.

444
00:34:22,780 --> 00:34:27,100
We don't want to become a group of people that settle for shallow, surface-level community.

445
00:34:27,100 --> 00:34:30,540
We want to get past small talk.

446
00:34:30,540 --> 00:34:34,980
We want to be vulnerable and transparent, and we want to be held accountable.

447
00:34:34,980 --> 00:34:46,960
We can't just get the first part right and share confessed sins but never actually do anything about it.

448
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:51,140
We have to also get to the point where we can hold one another accountable for the sin that we're confessing.

449
00:34:51,140 --> 00:34:54,540
You guys have heard churches use the phrase, it's okay to not be okay.

450
00:34:54,540 --> 00:34:56,500
It's just not okay to stay there.

451
00:34:56,500 --> 00:34:59,000
That's what we want to be.

452
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,280
We want to be a church where it's okay to not be okay.

453
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:02,300
Be vulnerable.

454
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:03,000
Be transparent.

455
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:06,420
Find a community of people that you trust and you can share your struggles with,

456
00:35:06,420 --> 00:35:07,820
and they're going to hold you accountable.

457
00:35:07,820 --> 00:35:11,000
They're not going to let you get away with you being selfish and sinful.

458
00:35:13,160 --> 00:35:14,580
So, let me challenge you with a few things.

459
00:35:14,580 --> 00:35:17,060
Be intentional to invest in people.

460
00:35:17,060 --> 00:35:20,140
Step out of your comfort zone.

461
00:35:20,140 --> 00:35:24,140
Spend time in the lobby before and after church getting to know people.

462
00:35:24,140 --> 00:35:27,140
Create a hospitable space.

463
00:35:27,140 --> 00:35:32,160
If you've got a house that's hospitable and welcoming, invite people into your home.

464
00:35:32,940 --> 00:35:36,560
If you're not blessed with that type of home, invite somebody to coffee.

465
00:35:36,560 --> 00:35:38,020
Meet them at a park.

466
00:35:38,020 --> 00:35:39,800
Create a hospitable space.

467
00:35:39,800 --> 00:35:42,860
Invite people to do life with you.

468
00:35:44,360 --> 00:35:48,480
I've developed, he's not here, so I'm going to pat him on the back.

469
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:52,240
Donovan Russell is really good at this.

470
00:35:52,240 --> 00:35:55,240
Donovan and I have developed a great friendship over the past year.

471
00:35:55,240 --> 00:36:00,820
And it's not because he's had the time and bandwidth to, like, create space for him and I to build friendship.

472
00:36:00,820 --> 00:36:03,240
It's because he just invites me to do life with him.

473
00:36:03,240 --> 00:36:05,640
He's like, hey, I'm going to my kid's soccer game.

474
00:36:05,640 --> 00:36:06,360
Come with me.

475
00:36:06,360 --> 00:36:11,100
You don't have to stop everything that you're doing to form Christian community.

476
00:36:11,100 --> 00:36:12,500
Invite people along with you.

477
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:14,060
And they're going to tell you no sometimes.

478
00:36:14,360 --> 00:36:15,280
Keep doing it.

479
00:36:15,280 --> 00:36:19,060
Keep inviting people into your home, creating space for them.

480
00:36:19,060 --> 00:36:26,400
Nothing facilitates authentic biblical community faster than eating meals together in a dirty house

481
00:36:26,400 --> 00:36:33,380
with crazy kids running around fighting and screaming and using bad words that they learned from me.

482
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:36,780
Because I don't yet have a parent filter.

483
00:36:36,780 --> 00:36:38,440
Okay?

484
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:40,860
This has been my experience.

485
00:36:40,860 --> 00:36:49,420
I have people in this congregation that have done life with me in their homes and nothing facilitates authentic biblical community faster than that.

486
00:36:49,420 --> 00:36:53,540
And don't settle for just the people that you feel comfortable with.

487
00:36:53,540 --> 00:36:58,260
3,000 people from every age and every ethnicity and every background.

488
00:36:58,380 --> 00:37:04,760
One of the most beautiful things about Christian community, when it's done right, is that it's the most diverse community on the planet.

489
00:37:04,760 --> 00:37:08,940
Because it's submitted to Jesus as King and Lord.

490
00:37:08,940 --> 00:37:18,420
And so, don't just feel comfortable with people who are in your same socioeconomic background, with your same color skin, with your same political ideologies.

491
00:37:18,640 --> 00:37:22,080
Do life with people that think differently than you.

492
00:37:22,080 --> 00:37:25,480
Alright, that was the bad news.

493
00:37:25,480 --> 00:37:28,040
What's the good news?

494
00:37:28,040 --> 00:37:33,880
So, although this might be the longest and hardest sermon application you will ever have,

495
00:37:33,880 --> 00:37:39,240
I believe it's also the most fulfilling and rewarding thing that will ever happen to you.

496
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:46,220
In the past nine months, Merce and I have gone through some of the hardest and difficult times of our life.

497
00:37:46,220 --> 00:37:55,820
But in those times, we have experienced some of the most life-giving and genuinely authentic community that I could ever imagine.

498
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:02,380
It's the most rewarding thing you'll ever experience.

499
00:38:02,380 --> 00:38:06,600
To have people that truly know you and truly love you in the midst of your brokenness.

500
00:38:06,600 --> 00:38:08,520
In the midst of your selfishness.

501
00:38:08,520 --> 00:38:12,240
And are unwilling to let you stay there.

502
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:21,960
Not only is it the most rewarding thing that you'll ever do, but like I said earlier, it is the most effective apologetic for this culture.

503
00:38:21,960 --> 00:38:24,060
Verse 47.

504
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:31,040
And each day, the Lord added to their fellowship those who were being saved.

505
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:32,640
Each day.

506
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:37,020
New believers were added daily.

507
00:38:37,020 --> 00:38:38,860
And the church grew.

508
00:38:38,860 --> 00:38:40,820
Not because it was perfect.

509
00:38:40,820 --> 00:38:45,620
Not because it had the best production or marketing strategy.

510
00:38:45,620 --> 00:38:48,820
Not because it had the most talented worship team or charismatic preacher.

511
00:38:48,820 --> 00:38:50,160
The church grew daily.

512
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:54,460
Because it was made up of individuals who were committed to core doctors.

513
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:57,600
Who were devoted to prayer.

514
00:38:57,600 --> 00:39:00,560
Who were submitted to the Holy Spirit.

515
00:39:00,560 --> 00:39:05,100
And who were invested in building authentic biblical community.

516
00:39:05,100 --> 00:39:10,480
I don't know about you, but this is the type of church that I want to be a part of.

517
00:39:10,480 --> 00:39:16,480
This is the church I want to see us develop here in this place for the long haul.

518
00:39:17,400 --> 00:39:24,340
May we be a people that look back a year from now and have a similar summary statement be true of our church.

519
00:39:26,240 --> 00:39:31,380
I'm not naive enough to believe that we won't have some of the other things that are sprinkled throughout the book of Acts.

520
00:39:31,380 --> 00:39:35,740
Arguments and fights and quarreling and difficulty.

521
00:39:35,740 --> 00:39:43,880
But in the midst of all of that us, that humanness, that us being real, may we have a summary statement like this be true of our church.

522
00:39:46,280 --> 00:39:54,540
And maybe I'm crazy, but I honestly believe if each of us as individuals, we get serious about these four core attitudes.

523
00:39:54,540 --> 00:40:00,180
We can see our own version of this in the city of Winchester in 2023.

524
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:03,180
And believers will be added to our number daily.

525
00:40:03,180 --> 00:40:14,240
Because who doesn't want to be known and loved in an authentic and diverse community of people centered around the author and perfecter of all things?

526
00:40:14,240 --> 00:40:20,140
Who doesn't want to see abundant life that Jesus offers us?

527
00:40:20,140 --> 00:40:29,560
Imagine a church committed to core doctrine, devoted to prayer, submitted to the Holy Spirit, and invested in building an authentic biblical community.

528
00:40:29,560 --> 00:40:30,240
This is my prayer.

529
00:40:30,240 --> 00:40:34,680
This is our prayer for our church moving forward.

530
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:38,360
We're going to transition to one final song.

531
00:40:38,360 --> 00:40:43,300
I asked Marissa to lead us in a hymn because I thought that would bless a lot of us.

532
00:40:43,300 --> 00:40:45,440
And we picked this hymn, Be Thou My Vision.

533
00:40:45,440 --> 00:40:47,240
Because again, let's bookend.

534
00:40:47,240 --> 00:40:51,300
The beginning of this message and the end of this message, we'll be entitled My Part Matters.

535
00:40:51,300 --> 00:40:57,740
Let's bookend it from the beginning and the end with the fact that it only matters if we're following after Him.

536
00:40:57,740 --> 00:41:01,960
May the Lord be our vision in this as we seek to obey Him.

537
00:41:03,280 --> 00:41:13,500
If we're saying I'll lead us in communion and then we will hopefully start one step into building authentic biblical community in the lobby and in the kids' room and in the parking lot.

538
00:41:13,500 --> 00:41:16,400
One step towards the church that God's calling us to be.
